{
 "slug": "moon-laser",
 "title": "How do we know how far away the Moon is?",
 "note": "LOOK TEST v3 (engine v2, 2D story world). Facts: Apache Point Observatory, New Mexico (32.78 N, 105.82 W) runs the APOLLO lunar laser ranging experiment; Apollo 11 left the first retroreflector in July 1969 (site 0.67 N, 23.47 E); mean Earth-Moon distance 384,400 km; light's round trip is about 2.5 s; only a handful of photons come back per pulse. The Earth-Moon shot is at TRUE scale (Earth 1000 units, Moon 272, distance 30,170).",
 "voice": "af_heart",
 "speed": 0.86,
 "lead": 1.0,
 "tail": 2.2,
 "music": {
  "file": "../studio/music/wonder_silver_blue_light.wav",
  "db": -22,
  "start": 6
 },
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "On a mountain in New Mexico, astronomers aim a *laser* at the Moon.",
   "sfx": [
    {
     "at": 3.0,
     "file": "public/sfx/beam_laser_1.wav",
     "db": -15
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The pulse of light leaves the Earth\u2026 and crosses three hundred and eighty-four thousand kilometres of *empty* space.",
   "gap": 0.7,
   "sfx": [
    {
     "at": 0.3,
     "file": "public/sfx/motion_whoosh_airy_1.wav",
     "db": -21
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "There, it finds a small *mirror*, left on the Moon by the Apollo astronauts in 1969.",
   "gap": 0.7
  },
  {
   "text": "Two and a half seconds later, a handful of photons come home\u2026 and that tiny delay tells us *exactly* how far away the Moon is.",
   "gap": 0.7,
   "sfx": [
    {
     "at": 0.2,
     "file": "public/sfx/machine_chime_1.wav",
     "db": -22
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
